If a set of natural numbers is generated by the generators, some set of points of Cantor space corresponds to it.

The generation hypothesis supplies a formula whose evaluation on the natural numbers is the given set, and the evaluation of that same formula on Cantor space is a corresponding set.

If two sets of natural numbers correspond to one set of points of Cantor space, the two sets of natural numbers are equal.

Each correspondence supplies a formula. The two formulas have the same evaluation on Cantor space, so they are semantically equivalent, and semantic equivalence equates their evaluations on the natural numbers.
